The Easy Version

There’s a trick to this. Let’s compare the interval width we have to the one we want.

\[
\begin{aligned}
\pm 0.037 &= \pm 1.96\sqrt{\frac{\hat\theta (1-\hat\theta)}{625}}  \\
\pm 0.01 &= \pm 1.96\sqrt{\frac{\hat\theta (1-\hat\theta)}{n}}   
\end{aligned}
\]

All that changes in this formula is the same size. And the sample size ratio falls out of the interval width ratio.

\[
\frac{0.037}{.01}
= \frac{1.96\sqrt{\frac{\hat\theta (1-\hat\theta)}{625}}}{1.96\sqrt{\frac{\hat\theta (1-\hat\theta)}{n}}}
= \sqrt{\frac{n}{625}}
\qqtext{ so } n = 625\left(\frac{0.037}{.01}\right)^2 \approx 8000
\]

To get the new sample size, multiply …

the sample size we have
the square of the desired ratio of the interval widths.

To double precision, quadruple the sample size. To triple it, increase it 9x.
To get another digit, i.e. increase precision 10x, increase the sample size 100x.

Review Exercise 1










Suppose we sample a point \((X, Y)\) in the plot above uniformly at random.

Ignore the jitter; just think of the points as being at \(X=0\) and \(X=1\).
What is \(\mathop{\mathrm{E}}\{ \mu(X) \}\)?




There are two ways of thinking about calculating \(\mathop{\mathrm{E}}\{\mu(X)\}\).

We just calculate the expectation of it, thinking of it as an arbitrary random variable.
We use the law of iterated expectations to show it’s the unconditional mean of \(Y\).


\[
\small{
\begin{aligned}
\mathop{\mathrm{E}}\qty{ \mu(X) } &= \frac{1}{2}\mu(0) + \frac{1}{2}\mu(1)= \frac{1}{2} \cdot 1 + \frac{1}{2} \cdot 1.25 = 1.125 && \text{ the first way } \\
\mathop{\mathrm{E}}\qty{ \mu(X) } &= \mathop{\mathrm{E}}\qty{\mathop{\mathrm{E}}\qty(Y \mid X)} = \mathop{\mathrm{E}}\qty{Y} = \frac{1}{6} \cdot 0.75 + \frac{1}{6} \cdot 1 + \ldots && \text{ the second way }
\end{aligned}
}
\]

